{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'rg -n \"''^(#|[0-9]+('\"\\\\.[0-9]+)* )|Table|Fig\\\\.|reduction|Evaluation|evaluat|Experiments|Conclusion|WCET\\\" verification/example_site/spaces/compilers/sources/artifacts/source-04-a-compiler-framework-for-the-reduction-of-worst-case-execution-times--5aee9841160e/source.md | head -n 220\"","aggregated_output":"1:# 04-a-compiler-framework-for-the-reduction-of-worst-case-execution-times\n9:A compiler framework for the reduction of worst-case\n24:bounds of the worst-case execution time (WCET) of a system during code generation\n25:and in systematically optimizing code to reduce WCET.\n26:   This article presents concepts and infrastructures for WCET-aware code gener-\n27:ation and optimization techniques for WCET reduction. All together, they help to\n32:Keywords Real-time · WCET · Compiler · Code generation · Optimization\n35:1 Introduction\n41:(ACETs). The effect of optimizations on worst-case execution times (WCETs) has not\n54:252                                                       Real-Time Syst (2010) 46: 251–300\n61:of timing, applied optimizations may lead to large WCET degradations. Therefore, it\n63:The code produced by the compiler is then manually fed into a WCET analyzer that\n66:in the hope that the resulting C and assembly codes have a lower WCET.\n67:    Up to now, no tools exist that assist the designer to purposively reduce WCETs\n70:able to have a WCET-aware compiler. Integrating WCET analysis into the compiler\n71:itself has the following benefits: first, it extends the compiler by a WCET timing\n74:WCET. Thus, the designer no longer needs to use unoptimized code, cheaper hard-\n76:and the tedious work of manually reducing the WCET of auto-generated C code is\n77:taken from the designer. Third, manual WCET analysis is no more required since this\n78:is done transparently by the compiler, using its tight integration of a WCET analyzer.\n79:    This article presents concepts, infrastructures and optimizations for WCET-aware\n81:gether form the WCET-aware C Compiler WCC (WCET-aware Compilation 2010),\n83:WCET reduction at both source code and assembly code level.\n85:1.1 Motivation\n91:it is in general very difficult or even impossible to determine the actual WCET of a\n93:the actual WCET, reliable upper bounds have to be determined by sound methods.\n94:    Two different approaches are used to estimate WCET bounds. The first approach\n95:is measurement-based WCET analysis. Here, the program under analysis is executed\n98:WCET. This approach is highly unsafe since no guarantee can be deduced that the\n100:    If safe WCET guarantees for hard real-time systems are needed, static program\n101:analyses are used. The overall workflow of the leading static WCET analyzer aiT\n105:Fig. 1 Workflow of the static\n106:WCET analyzer aiT\n111:Fig. 2 Original example CFG\n116:(AbsInt Angewandte Informatik GmbH 2010) is shown in Fig. 1. aiT applies sta-\n119:sis stage computes a program’s global WCET. For each block on a path P from a\n122:number of executions C is estimated. The WCET of P is the sum of the products\n123:T ∗ C over all blocks of P . A program’s WCET is computed by finding the maxi-\n124:mum path WCET for all feasible paths. This maximization problem is modeled and\n127:WCET is called the worst-case execution path (WCEP). Hence, the WCET of a pro-\n128:gram is equal to the WCET of its WCEP. In the following, a path’s WCET will also\n129:be called the path’s length. To reduce WCETs by a WCET-aware compiler, optimiza-\n132:not shorten the WCEP and thus do not reduce the WCET. Therefore, optimization\n133:strategies for WCET reduction must have detailed knowledge about the WCEP. Sta-\n134:tic WCET analysis as shown above provides information about a program’s WCEP,\n135:but solely knowing the WCEP is still insufficient for effective WCET reduction.\n136:    Consider the CFG of a function main in Fig. 2 that consists of five basic blocks.\n137:Each of them has the indicated WCET given in processor cycles. As can be seen, the\n139:arrows in the figure, leads to an overall WCET of 205 cycles.\n141:254                                                      Real-Time Syst (2010) 46: 251–300\n143:Fig. 3 CFG after optimization\n149:   We assume that some WCET-aware optimization reduces the WCET of basic\n150:block b, that lies on the WCEP, from 80 cycles down to 40 cycles (cf. Fig. 3). As\n152:b’s WCET by 40 cycles does not reduce the overall WCET by 40 cycles. Instead, the\n153:overall WCET now amounts to 195 cycles which corresponds to an overall saving of\n157:timization. Thus, a WCET-aware compiler is faced with the following challenges\n158:which turn the development of WCET-aware optimizations into an even more de-\n161:– During the entire optimization process, WCET-aware optimizations must have de-\n165:– Additionally, optimization decisions should not only rely on local WCET data for a\n166:  single code block, since local WCET savings for a single block do not necessarily\n167:  translate into global WCET savings of the same order of magnitude.\n170:WCET-aware code generation. The key contributions are that the proposed compiler\n172:– is equipped with a precise WCET timing model during code optimization,\n173:– applies static WCET analysis automatically in the background, without requiring\n174:  the compiler user to reason about assembly code structures that influence WCET\n175:  analysis. Instead, the user is urged to support WCET analysis at source code level,\n176:– features various optimizations which are explicitly tailored towards WCET reduc-\n178:– applies WCET optimizations both at source code and at assembly code level. This\n188:1.2 Related work\n190:A very first approach to integrate WCET techniques into a compiler was presented in\n191:Börjesson (1996). Flow facts used for WCET analysis were annotated manually via\n194:8051 which is an inherently simple and predictable machine without pipeline and\n205:mizations. Their approach was thoroughly tested and led to precise WCET estimates.\n206:However, compilation and WCET analysis are done in a decoupled way. The assem-\n207:bly file generated by the compiler is passed to the WCET analyzer together with the\n210:   In Zhao et al. (2004, 2005), the integration of a proprietarily developed WCET\n213:which computes the WCET of paths, loops and functions and passes this data back\n214:to the compiler. This approach has the following limitations. First, the WCET an-\n215:alyzer works with very coarse granularity since it only computes WCETs of paths,\n216:loops and functions. WCETs for basic blocks or single instructions are unavailable.\n218:Second, WCET-related data which is not the WCET itself is unavailable, too. This\n220:dicted cache behavior etc. Finally, WCET optimization at higher levels of abstraction\n221:like e.g., source code level is infeasible since WCET-related data is not provided at\n224:1.3 Overall compiler infrastructure and article outline\n226:The WCET-aware C Compiler WCC (WCET-aware Compilation 2010) described in\n229:in Fig. 4. Those modules of the compiler connected with solid arrows resemble a\n234:256                                                      Real-Time Syst (2010) 46: 251–300\n236:Fig. 4 WCC compiler\n258:   The key components which turn WCC into a unique WCET-aware C compiler are\n259:depicted with dashed arrows in Fig. 4. Sections 2–6 describe these modules in more\n261:WCET analyzer, flow facts, loop analyzer and back-annotation.\n262:   Based on this infrastructure, WCET-aware source code level optimizations (proce-\n263:dure cloning and positioning) are presented in Sects. 7–8, followed by WCET-aware\n269:2 Specification of memory hierarchies\n275:the software on the other hand. Obviously, the WCET estimates produced by a static\n276:WCET analyzer as described in Sect. 1.1 also heavily depend on the memories.\n277:    In the compil