  "source_dossier": {
    "summary_short": "This source presents WCC, a C compiler framework designed to optimize hard real-time software for worst-case execution time rather than average-case speed. Its central idea is to integrate static WCET analysis into compilation, lift timing information across compiler abstraction levels, and use that information to drive source-level and assembly-level optimizations.",
    "summary_long": "Falk and Lokuciejewski frame WCC as a response to a practical real-time-systems problem: ordinary compilers optimize primarily for average-case behavior, while industrial real-time workflows often disable optimizations and perform WCET analysis only after code generation. The paper argues that this separation is both costly and technically weak, because WCET reduction depends on knowing the worst-case execution path and on understanding how local changes affect global timing. WCC therefore integrates the aiT static WCET analyzer into the compiler, translates low-level compiler IR into aiT’s CRL2 format, invokes aiT transparently, imports timing results, and back-annotates those results to source-level ICD-C structures. This infrastructure supports automated loop-bound analysis, source-level optimizations such as procedure cloning and positioning, and assembly-level optimizations such as scratchpad allocation and WCET-aware register allocation. The evidence base is largely benchmark-driven. The loop analyzer handles 707 loops across 96 benchmarks with 99% analyzability and 96% exactness, while slicing and polyhedral evaluation reduce analysis time sharply on selected examples. Procedure cloning can reduce WCET estimates dramatically when call contexts make loop bounds constant, though the paper is candid about code-size expansion. Procedure positioning improves cache behavior using WCET-derived call frequencies. ILP-based code and data scratchpad allocation reduce WCET by prioritizing regions and variables with high worst-case benefit. Register allocation avoids spill code on the WCEP and gives the largest average reduction among the reported individual optimizations. The final matmult experiment shows that several optimizations can compose, reducing WCET from 775,720 cycles to 227,794 cycles. The main limitation is that the results are tied to the WCC/TriCore/aiT environment and to benchmark suites, but the paper provides a coherent architecture and quantitative evidence for WCET-aware compilation as a system-level approach.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Problem and Motivation",
        "body": "The paper’s motivating claim is that hard real-time development cannot rely on average-case compiler optimization or late manual timing checks. WCET reduction is path-sensitive: optimizing a block or a noncritical path may not reduce the global bound. This makes the compiler’s lack of timing knowledge a root cause rather than a minor tooling gap.",
        "grounding_claim_ids": [
          "0"
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "Compiler-Analyzer Integration",
        "body": "WCC addresses the tooling gap by embedding aiT into compilation. The compiler converts its low-level representation into CRL2, invokes aiT without making the user manage analyzer setup, and imports value, loop-bound, cache, pipeline, and path-analysis results. This turns WCET analysis from a separate post-build activity into an optimization input.",
        "grounding_claim_ids": [
          "1"
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "Back-Annotation as Bridge",
        "body": "A central architectural move is back-annotation from assembly-level structures to source-level ICD-C structures. The paper treats this mapping carefully because basic blocks do not always correspond one-to-one across IRs. Once mapped, WCC can expose WCEP membership, edge execution frequencies, cache misses, code size, and spill-code data to high-level transformations.",
        "grounding_claim_ids": [
          "2"
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "Flow Facts and Loop Bounds",
        "body": "Static WCET analysis needs safe loop iteration bounds, and manually supplying them is tedious and error-prone. WCC’s loop analyzer substantially automates this part: the reported benchmark table covers 707 loops with 99% analyzability and 96% exactness. Slicing and polyhedral evaluation then address analysis cost, with major runtime reductions on examples such as fft and matmul.",
        "grounding_claim_ids": [
          "3",
          "4"
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "Source-Level Optimizations",
        "body": "Procedure cloning and positioning show why WCET information is useful above assembly level. Cloning specializes functions so data-dependent loop bounds become constant in important call contexts, improving WCET precision but increasing code size. Positioning reorders functions using WCET-derived call frequencies to improve instruction-cache behavior without relying on potentially unrepresentative profiling inputs.",
        "grounding_claim_ids": [
          "5",
          "6"
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "Memory Allocation Optimizations",
        "body": "The scratchpad-memory results show the value of assembly-level optimization guided by WCET data. The code allocator chooses beneficial basic-block regions for fast program SPM, while the data allocator chooses frequently accessed variables for fast data SPM. Reported average reductions are substantial, and the code-size or solver overhead is described as negligible in the evaluated setting.",
        "grounding_claim_ids": [
          "7",
          "8"
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "Register Allocation and Composition",
        "body": "The register allocator deliberately protects the WCEP from spill code, even if that can increase total text size. Across 46 benchmarks it reduces WCET by 31.2% on average versus traditional graph coloring. The matmult sequence then demonstrates practical composition: register allocation, scratchpad allocation, and procedure positioning together cut the reported WCET by more than 70%.",
        "grounding_claim_ids": [
          "9",
          "10"
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "Assumptions and Limits",
        "body": "The paper’s strongest claims are grounded in a specific toolchain and target: WCC, aiT, and Infineon TriCore processors. The results therefore support the feasibility and effectiveness of this integrated architecture, but they should not be read as architecture-independent speedups. Several optimizations also involve trade-offs, especially analysis time and code-size growth.",
        "grounding_claim_ids": [
          "1",
          "5",
          "7",
          "8",
          "9"
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}

