      "structure_type": "wiki",
      "sections": [
        {
          "heading": "Shared concept",
          "content": "Across the sources, compiler improvements are presented as integrated infrastructures rather than isolated transformations. Profile-guided optimization adds profiling, super-block formation, and profile-aware versions of classic optimizations; machine-learning compilation adds training and deployment stages around the compiler; WCC connects a C compiler to aiT and composes multiple WCET-aware optimizations; distributed quantum compilation maps algorithms to constrained distributed architectures through strategy-specific compilation of remote operations."
        },
        {
          "heading": "Classical and learned compiler pipelines",
          "content": "Profile-guided classic optimization is explicitly complementary to conventional optimization, reformulating classic scalar transformations for super-blocks and super-block loops. Machine-learning compilation similarly inserts a learned model into the compiler after offline training, making optimization decisions for new programs rather than replacing the rest of the compiler pipeline."
        },
        {
          "heading": "WCET-aware composition",
          "content": "WCC provides a strong infrastructure example because it couples static analysis to compilation, raises timing facts back to source-level structures, accelerates loop analysis through slicing and polyhedral evaluation, and composes register allocation, code scratchpad allocation, data scratchpad allocation, and procedure positioning. The matmult case shows these optimizations can combine into a much larger WCET reduction than an isolated pass would suggest."
        },
        {
          "heading": "Domain-specific objectives",
          "content": "The sources target different objectives: average speed, learned heuristic selection, worst-case execution time, and distributed quantum feasibility or depth. A synthesis page can use this contrast to explain how modern compiler frameworks embed domain-specific evidence and passes while preserving a broader compiler workflow."
